%% ============================================================================
%% DarkFrameAnalyzer.pidoc — PixInsight reference documentation, English
%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% Compile with the Documentation Compiler script (command-line mode is
%% driven by scripts/build-script-doc.sh). The generated page is versioned
%% in doc/scripts/DarkFrameAnalyzer/ and deployed by the update package.
%% The French page lives in DarkFrameAnalyzer-fr.pidoc; keep both in sync.
%% ============================================================================

\documentclass PIScriptDoc

\script DarkFrameAnalyzer

\keywords { dark frames, calibration, master dark, outlier detection, hot pixels, WBPP }

\author { Caelo Works }

\copyright { 2026 Caelo Works }

\brief { Analyzes a series of dark frames and flags the ones that do not
belong with the rest of the series, before they reach the master dark. }

\description {

\xref ../DarkFrameAnalyzer-fr/DarkFrameAnalyzer-fr.html { Version française de cette documentation }

A master dark is a statistical combination of a series of individual darks.
It assumes that all the frames of the series describe the same sensor, at the
same temperature, with the same exposure time and the same read conditions. A
single frame that breaks this assumption, because the cooling had not settled,
because a light leak reached the sensor, or because the camera returned a
corrupt frame, degrades the master dark that every light frame is then
calibrated with.

Dark Frame Analyzer measures each frame of a series and compares it with the
series itself. For every dark it computes a sigma-clipped median, an exact
median absolute deviation derived from the 16-bit histogram, the number of hot
and saturated pixels, the deviation between the temperature setpoint and the
sensor temperature read from the FITS header, and the gradient between the
center of the frame and its corners. It then classifies every frame as valid,
as an alert, or as rejected, and reports both a color-coded table and a
detailed console report.

The script reads the frames, and never modifies them. It writes nothing unless
you ask for it: see \lref privacy { Privacy, network and files } below.

}

\usage {

The script is available from the \s { Script > CaeloWorks > DarkFrameAnalyzer }
menu item.

\subsection { Adding dark frames } {

\s Darks opens a file dialog to select individual frames, and \s Directory
adds every dark found in a directory. FITS (\c { .fits }, \c { .fit }, \c { .fts }) and
XISF (\c { .xisf }) frames are accepted, in uppercase or lowercase. \s Remove
drops the selected rows and \s { Clear all } empties the list.

All the frames of one series should be added at once: the statistical part of
the detection compares each frame with the others, so a list that mixes two
different series, or two temperatures, produces meaningless alerts.

}

\subsection { Detection thresholds } {

The \s { Detection thresholds } panel holds two kinds of criterion, and both
are applied to every frame.

\list [spaced] {

{ \s { Absolute criteria } compare a frame with a fixed value: the maximum
deviation between the temperature setpoint and the sensor temperature, in
degrees Celsius; the ADU level above which a pixel counts as hot; the maximum
number of saturated pixels tolerated per frame; and the maximum gradient, in
ADU, between the center and the corners of the frame. They do not depend on
the rest of the series. }

{ \s { Statistical criteria } compare a frame with its series: a sensitivity
in sigma units for the median, the noise, the hot pixel count and the
uniformity, plus the absolute deviations, in ADU, above which a deviation of
the median or of the noise becomes an alert or a rejection. They need at least
three readable frames; below that, the script reports that the statistical
detection has been skipped, and applies the absolute criteria only. }

}

The thresholds persist across sessions. \s Defaults restores the values
calibrated for IMX585-class sensors that the script ships with.

}

\subsection { Running the analysis } {

\s Analyze measures every frame of the list. The interface is locked while the
analysis runs, and \s Cancel interrupts it: the frames already measured are
kept, and the console reports how many frames were processed out of the total.

}

\subsection { Reading the results table } {

\table [header] {
   { { Column } { Contents } }
   { { \s &#35; } { Position of the frame in the list. } }
   { { \s File } { File name of the frame. } }
   { { \s Temp. } { Sensor temperature read from the FITS header, in degrees
     Celsius. } }
   { { \s Median } { Sigma-clipped median of the frame, in ADU: its thermal
     signal. } }
   { { \s Noise } { Median absolute deviation of the frame, in ADU: its read
     noise. } }
   { { \s { Hot px } } { Number of pixels above the hot pixel threshold. } }
   { { \s Sat. } { Number of saturated pixels. } }
   { { \s { &Delta; corn. } } { Difference between the median of the center of
     the frame and the median of its corners, in ADU: amp glow, light leaks
     and other spatial gradients. } }
   { { \s Status } { \s Valid, \s Alert, \s Rejected, or \s Error when the
     frame could not be read. } }
}

A row marked \s Alert deviates from the series without being unusable: look at
it, and decide. A row marked \s Rejected should be kept out of the
integration. Clicking a column header sorts the table; the \s Temp. and
\s { &Delta; corn. } columns are sorted as text, because they carry a sign, so
use the CSV export when the order of those two columns matters.

}

\subsection { Console report } {

The console receives a table of the per-frame metrics, the reference values
computed for the series, the reason why each frame has been flagged, and a
summary of the valid, alerted and rejected frames. It is the complete record
of the analysis, and it can be copied out of PixInsight as plain text.

}

\subsection { CSV export } {

\s { Export CSV... } writes every metric of the last analysis to a CSV file,
at the location you choose in a save dialog. The headers are fixed English
identifiers, whatever the language of the interface, so that the file can be
read by other tools.

}

\subsection { WBPP exclusions } {

\s { WBPP exclusions... } opens the list of the frames that should be kept out
of the integration: the rejected frames, and also the alerted ones when
\s { Include alerts } is checked. From that dialog, \s { Export .txt... }
writes the list to a text file, one path per line, at a location you choose,
and \s { Move to rejected/... } moves the listed frames to a \c rejected
subdirectory created next to them, so that WBPP no longer sees them. The move
asks for a confirmation first, and the frames are moved, never deleted.

}

\subsection { Interface language } {

The interface is available in English and French, selected from the language
list of the dialog. The choice persists across sessions. The headers of the
CSV export are unaffected.

}

}

\section { \label privacy Privacy, network and files } {

\subsection { Network } {

The script makes no network connection of its own. It downloads nothing,
uploads nothing, and contains no telemetry: nothing is measured, counted or
transmitted about you or about your frames.

One action involves the network, and only when you ask for it: clicking the
\e { by CaeloWorks } line in the header of the dialog opens the page of this
script on the developer website, at
\xref https://pixinsight-scripts.caelo.works/en/scripts/dark-frame-analyzer
{ pixinsight-scripts.caelo.works }, in the web browser configured on your
system. The full URL is shown in the tooltip of that line before you click it,
and follows the language of the interface.

}

\subsection { External programs } {

To open that page, and only then, the script asks your operating system to
open the URL with your default web browser. It runs \c { cmd /c start } on
Windows, \c { /usr/bin/open } on macOS and \c { xdg-open } on Linux, with the URL as
its only argument. No other program is ever launched, no program is
distributed with the script, and nothing is launched without an explicit click
from you.

}

\subsection { Files written outside the PixInsight installation } {

The script never writes inside the PixInsight installation, and never modifies
your dark frames. Outside the installation it writes only on an explicit
action, and only where you tell it to:

\list [spaced] {

{ The \s { CSV report } of the analysis, at the path selected in the save
dialog of \s { Export CSV... } . }

{ The \s { exclusion list } , a \c { .txt } file, at the path selected in the save
dialog of the \s { WBPP exclusions... } dialog. }

{ A \s { \c rejected subdirectory } next to the frames to exclude, created by
\s { Move to rejected/... } in that same dialog, after a confirmation.
The rejected frames are moved into it; no file is deleted. }

}

}

\subsection { Settings } {

The detection thresholds and the language of the interface are stored through
the settings interface of PixInsight, under the \c DarkFrameAnalyzer key of
the settings of the application. The script reads and writes nothing else
there, and touches no setting belonging to PixInsight or to another product.

}

}

\section { Requirements and limitations } {

\list [spaced] {

{ PixInsight 1.9.0 or newer, on Windows, macOS and Linux. }

{ FITS (\c { .fits }, \c { .fit }, \c { .fts }) and XISF (\c { .xisf }) dark frames. }

{ The sensor temperature and the setpoint are read from the FITS header. A
frame whose header carries neither cannot be checked for thermal drift. }

{ The statistical criteria need at least three readable frames. With fewer,
the script says so explicitly and applies the absolute criteria only. }

{ The \s Temp. and \s { &Delta; corn. } columns of the table are sorted as
text, because no readable form of a signed number sorts correctly as text in a
PixInsight tree box. Use the CSV export to order a series on those two
metrics. }

}

}

\make
